  "research": {
    "title": "Supermarket Checkout Laser Scanner",
    "year": "1974",
    "by": "Spectra-Physics (first commercial: Marsh Supermarkets, Troy, OH); later IBM, NCR, Symbol",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Input", "Retail", "Scanning", "Object-as-Input"],
    "overview": "The fixed-position supermarket laser scanner replaced the act of typing prices with the act of swiping a physical item over a glass window. The first commercial deployment was the Spectra-Physics Model A installed at a Marsh supermarket in Troy, Ohio on 26 June 1974, projecting a helium-neon laser beam up through a glass plate; the cashier passed each item's UPC label across the window and the machine decoded it with a beep. It made checkout a physical ritual: lift, swipe, beep, repeat. The interface has no keyboard, no buttons, no screen to read — the object itself is the input, offered to a fixed scanning surface, and the entire feedback channel is a single tone.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "The checkout beep",
        "text": "The interaction model inverted the cash register. Before, the register was a keyboard the cashier typed into. The fixed scanner turned the checkout counter itself into a sensing surface: the cashier becomes an alignment operator, presenting each product to the window at the right orientation. The machine's only answer is a beep — success. It is feedback reduced to a single bit, and it worked for a generation."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Origin and rollout",
        "text": "The idea came from RCA's 1972 bullseye-code prototype, but the practical system was Spectra-Physics' laser scanner reading the UPC barcode. After the 1974 Troy, Ohio install, scanners spread through the late 1970s and 1980s, with IBM, NCR, and later Symbol Technologies building the fixed and handheld units that became ubiquitous. The fixed glass-window scanner remains the checkout interaction paradigm worldwide."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why it matters to HCI",
        "text": "It is a pure case of object-as-input: no command language, no buttons — the physical good IS the instruction. And it is the rare input device whose feedback is a single audio beep standing in for an entire transaction's correctness. Decades before 'scannable objects' became a design concept, the supermarket scanner made every packaged good a barcode-bearing input token."
      }
    ],
